[Title "Pawn Breakthrough: Capablanca's Method"]
[Elo "950"]
[Theme "Pawn Endgame, Breakthrough, King Activity"]
[Objective "March the White King to the queenside and execute the b4 pawn sacrifice to shatter Black's defensive wall."]
[Intro "In this famous endgame study, Capablanca demonstrates a fundamental pawn breakthrough. Black's pawns look solid, but White can tear them apart with a single well-timed sacrifice. First, the King must find the right square."]
[Mode "lecture"]
[FinalReflection "The b4 sacrifice is the heart of this lesson. One pawn given up opens the entire queenside, creates a passed pawn, and leaves Black helpless. Always look for pawn breaks that cannot be met without creating a new weakness."]
[MastersNote "Pawn endings are won by the side that creates a passed pawn first. The King must be active — it is a fighting piece in the endgame."]

1. Kd3 { Activate the King immediately. In pawn endgames the King is your strongest piece — march it toward the action. } 1... Kd7 2. Kc3 { Continue heading queenside. White is aiming for a3, where the King supports the coming b4 break. } 2... Kc6 3. Kb2 { Steady progress. Every King step brings the breakthrough closer. } 3... Kb7 4. Ka3 { The King reaches its ideal post. From a3 it controls b4 and will recapture after Black takes. } 4... Ka6 5. b4! { The decisive breakthrough sacrifice. White shatters Black's entire queenside defensive structure in one move. Declining with 5...cxb4+ leads to a lost King-and-pawn ending for Black after Kb3. } 5... axb4+ 6. Kb3 { Recapture the initiative. The White King now becomes the attacking force. } 6... Ka5 7. Kb2 { Manoeuvre — the King retreats to prepare a2-b2-a2 or simply follow the passed pawn. } 7... Kxa4 8. Ka2 { The White King stays close, preparing to shepherd the c-pawn or stop any counter-play. } 8... b3+ 9. Kb2 { The only square. White simply waits for Black to run out of useful moves. } 9... Kb4 10. Kb1 { Stepping aside. Black's b-pawn is frozen, and the c-pawn will now advance. } 10... Kxc4 1-0
